.TH NIHILURK 6 "October 2026" "nihilurk 0.3.0" "Games"
.SH NAME
nihilurk \- terminal roguelike
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nihilurk
.RI [ name | save ]
[\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-seed\fR \fIseed\fR] [\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-\-centered\fR]
[\fB\-ns\fR|\fB\-\-no\-save\fR] [\fB\-nb\fR|\fB\-\-no\-blood\fR]
[\fB\-nshake\fR|\fB\-\-no\-shake\fR] [\fB\-nobones\fR|\fB\-\-no\-bones\fR]
[\fB\-endless\fR|\fB\-\-endless\fR]
[\fB\-anim\-rate\fR|\fB\-\-anim\-rate\fR \fImultiplier\fR]
[\fB\-b\fR|\fB\-\-body\fR \fIbody\fR | \fB\-am\fR|\fB\-\-as\-monster\fR \fIspecies\fR]
.PP
.B nihilurk
\fB\-content\fR | \fB\-\-content\fR
.PP
.B nihilurk
\fB\-scores\fR | \fB\-\-leaderboard\fR
.PP
.B nihilurk
\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-help\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nihilurk
is a turn-based terminal roguelike: exploration, combat, and survival in a
thirteen-floor dungeon, drawn on a grid of character cells and answered one
keystroke at a time.
.PP
To win, descend to the thirteenth floor, take the
.I "Element of Yoord"
that waits there, and carry it up and out of the dungeon. Once you carry it,
the only way out is up, and the creatures you meet may be drawn from anywhere
in the dungeon. Few runs come back up. Death is permanent; there is no retry
and no resurrection.
.PP
What sets the game apart is the trick shot. A missile or a blast that lands on
a trap you can see, on a coin, or on a potion lying on the floor sets it off
from a distance, over every tile around it, and the burst can set off the next
one. A creature standing on something you could set off is drawn on a magenta
cell. Only ranged attacks do it, and it catches you too if you stand close.
.PP
Spirits are the shops. They are
.B &
creatures, and you make a deal with one by walking into it. Some give away a
choice of items; others trade if you carry something they want. The game has
no currency. Dealing with them too much, or hurting them, upsets the balance
and turns every spirit hostile. A scroll of atonement makes them peaceful
again.
You carry a hidden alignment from \-3 to +3 between two camps of spirit; each
spirit that leaves the floor moves it one step toward that spirit's camp, and
the word BALANCE under the status line shows it. Gaining a Helper moves it up
one, and blowing one up moves it down two. Reach either end and the spirits
turn.
.PP
If you have played other roguelikes, some things you expect are missing or
different. You cannot rest, search or pass a turn; stairs are the only
recovery that is not magic. There is no hunger; the Dungeon Lord's patience
runs out floor by floor instead. There are no experience levels: nihil grows
by finding things and the lurk by killing them. You know what every potion and
scroll is the moment you find it; only the quality of gear is hidden. There is
no start-up menu, as in Rogue; your name and your body are arguments. Throw a
treat at a monster and it may become your Helper; you keep one at a time, and other charmed creatures do not follow you between floors. Throw a wand and it spends
every charge at once. A coin is spent the moment you step on it, or you can
shoot it.
.PP
The game needs a terminal of at least 80 columns by 25 rows. In a smaller one
the message log draws garbled letters; that is a known limit and will not be
fixed.
.PP
nihilurk speaks English and a bit of Portuguese (pt). Spanish (es) and Haitian
Creole (ht) exist in the codebase but need help. The installed
.B nihilurk
command picks the language from
.BR LC_ALL ,
.B LANG
or
.BR LANGUAGE ,
and falls back to English if none matches. To choose one yourself, put
.B \-\-lang
.I pt
first. From a clone, a language is a build choice with no flag:
.B cargo run \-p nihilurk \-\-no\-default\-features \-\-features lang\-pt
.PP
To get started you only need to know two things: the direction keys move you,
and walking into a monster attacks it. The rest of the commands are listed
below.
.PP
The status line keeps the numbers you need at a glance: health, magic, attack,
defence, depth, and score. The message log beneath the map describes what has
just happened; when
.B \-\-MORE\-\-
appears, press Space or Enter to continue reading.
.PP
The Dungeon Lord allows only so much hesitation on each floor. Stay too long
and a portal carries you to another depth whether you are ready or not.
.PP
The game uses one save file,
.IR name .sav ,
in the directory nihilurk was started from. Quitting saves the current run unless
.B \-ns
is used, and starting the game again with the same name continues it. While
time is stopped nothing is saved: the game saves as time stops and again as it
starts, and quitting in between asks twice and loses those turns. A run
that ends in death is deleted. A completed run is retained as clear data:
beginning another journey on it asks first, because the Element returns to
the Dungeon Lord, and declining leaves the world its light.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-help ", " \-\-help
Print a short command-line guide and exit without entering the alternate
screen. This page is the full reference.
.TP
.BI "\-s, \-\-seed" " seed"
Use
.I seed
as the run's unsigned 64-bit seed. The seed fixes the dungeon's maps: the
walls of a floor are the same every time that floor of that seed is entered,
today, tomorrow, and after a reload. Monsters, items, and traps are rolled
afresh on each entry, so a familiar corridor may hold unfamiliar company, and
two runs on one seed that take the same staircases see the same everything.
An unparseable or missing value is refused as an invalid argument.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-centered
Centre the map on the player instead of using the fixed viewport.
.TP
.BR \-ns ", " \-\-no\-save
Do not write a save file. The run leaves nothing behind, on quit or on
victory.
.TP
.BR \-nb ", " \-\-no\-blood
Disable bloodstains and the corpse-and-bones death animation.
.TP
.BR \-nshake ", " \-\-no\-shake
Disable screen shake. The map never leaves its moorings;
.B \-anim\-rate
can only slow a shake, not still one.
.TP
.BR \-nobones ", " \-\-no\-bones
Skip the bones mechanic: a death never writes a
.BR bones\-N.sav ,
and an ascent never reads one.
.TP
.BR \-endless ", " \-\-endless
No Element of Yoord ever spawns, so there is no way to win. The dungeon keeps
going down past the thirteenth floor.
.TP
.BI "\-anim\-rate, \-\-anim\-rate" " multiplier"
Set the on-screen hold time for particle, mapping, and shake animation
frames. The default is 1.0; raise it for a terminal whose redraw cannot keep
up, lower it for snappier animations. The value is clamped to 0.1 through 5.0;
an unparseable or missing value is refused as an invalid argument.
.TP
.BI "\-b, \-\-body" " body"
Which creature descends:
.B nihil
(the default) or
.BR lurk .
Nihil carries gear and gets stronger by wearing what turns up. Nihil plays
forward, with few choices and plain ones: the body to start with. Nihil is
born knowing the charge (see
.B Shift + direction
below).
The lurk is quadruped, fanged, clawed and furred, and draws as a magenta
.BR @ .
It has seven hit points to nihil's twelve and two magic to nihil's four, it
carries nothing, and the only thing it can ever put on is a ring \(em its
claws are its weapon and its fur is its armour. In exchange it is
.BR quick ,
half again as fast as anything else in the dungeon; it moves unnoticed until
it is within reach; a step toward a creature across one empty tile lunges for
a sure hit at triple the roll, and each landed blow winds up the next; it knows Bide and pays for it; and one
creature in seven that dies on the floor makes it stronger in one of its four
numbers, at random. A wand of cancellation takes what a lurk has and not what
it is: the shape, the tempo and the rule about rings survive it. The lurk
plays slower than nihil: too thin to trade blows, it asks for a decision every
fight, and since it wears nothing but rings its run rides on potions,
scrolls, wands and coins, each spent once. Shift still runs, but it cannot
charge.
.IP
.B \-b
and
.B \-am
are the same choice asked two ways and may not be given together.
.TP
.BI "\-am, \-\-as\-monster" " species"
Play as a monster. The run starts in that species' body: its glyph, hit
points, attack and armour dice, tempo, and the magic it was born with, taken
from the same bestiary row the dungeon builds its own monsters from. The name
must be one
.B \-content
lists under monsters; anything else is refused before the game starts.
.IP
You keep your own side of the fight, your viewshed, your pack and your score,
and you start with the pack empty \(em the usual kit is nihil's own. Only a
species that uses items can equip any, and innate magic that is a spell (a
dragon's fireball) sits in the spell bar and costs nothing to cast. The body
is written to the save and restored on load \(em the player's name is the
species, which is how the save knows. Two argument shapes are refused up front
so that stays true: a
.I name
that is a species (use
.B \-am
instead), and
.B \-am
given together with a save to load, which already knows what body it is in.
.TP
.BR \-content ", " \-\-content
Print every content name accepted by
.BR NIHILURK_SPAWN ,
grouped by category, and exit without entering the alternate screen, so the
output pipes into
.BR grep (1)
and
.BR less (1)
like any other listing.
.TP
.BR \-scores ", " \-\-leaderboard
Print the leaderboard \(em the ten highest scores ever recorded, one per line
as
.BR "RANK. NAME - OUTCOME - SCORE (WHEN)" ,
highest first \(em and exit without entering the alternate screen. Read from
.B leaderboard.sav
in the current directory, a small file in the same format as a save. Updated
whenever a run ends in a win or a death;
.B OUTCOME
is
.BR WIN ,
.B "LOSE (Asc.)"
for a death carrying the Element back out, or
.B "LOSE (Desc.)"
for a death on the way down;
.B WHEN
is the UTC date and time the run ended.
.SH POSITIONAL ARGUMENT
The name or save file is the
.B first
argument or it is not one at all: everything after it must be a flag. An
unrecognised argument in any later position is refused rather than read as a
name.
.TP
.I name
Start a new run under this player name. The name is uppercased in the game,
and the save file is lowercased from it.
.TP
.I save
Load a save file if the argument names an existing file, or if appending
.B .sav
names one. Matching is case-insensitive. If the save is clear data, a won
run, the game asks before spending it.
.PP
An unrecognised argument that starts with a dash, and a flag whose value is
missing or bad, are refused with
.BR "invalid argument ARG. Please see -help for more information" ,
and a player name cannot start with a dash. Options may appear in any order; a later positional
argument replaces an earlier one.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NIHILURK_SPAWN
A comma-separated list of exact content names. Each recognised name is
spawned on the nearest free walkable tile around the player whenever a floor
is built. Empty entries and unknown names are ignored. The setting applies to
every floor, including floors revisited on the way back up. Gear and
ammunition accept dressing around the name, in this order:
.RB [ cursed ]
.RB [ +N | \-N ]
.I name
.RB [ xN ].
.B cursed
adds a curse,
.BR +N / \-N
adds N to a weapon's hit, armour's guard or a launcher's throw, or sets the
whole number of a numeric ring (protection, strength, increase damage,
sharpshooting), and
.B xN
sets a stack size, clamped to the stack limit of 13. A curse and a minus are
independent. A modifier the item cannot use is ignored. Use
.B \-content
to discover names:
.sp
.EX
nihilurk -content | grep -i dragon
NIHILURK_SPAWN="dragon,bow,arrow" nihilurk
NIHILURK_SPAWN="cursed -2 long sword,+3 ring mail,arrow x13" nihilurk
.EE
.TP
.B NIHILURK_LEVEL
Make every floor one kind of special level instead of rolling for it:
.BR battlefield ,
.BR labyrinth ,
.BR vault ,
.BR bee
(or
.BR beeworld ),
.BR castle
or
.BR island .
Case does not matter. An unrecognised value is ignored. A save rebuilds its
map from the seed, so load it with the value it was written with.
.TP
.B NIHILURK_MAGICMAP
Select the style of the magic-mapping reveal animation instead of rolling one.
Row by row:
.BR row ,
.BR rows ,
.BR rowbyrow ,
.BR row-by-row ,
and
.BR curtain ;
spiral:
.BR spiral
and
.BR swirl ;
explode:
.BR explode ,
.BR explosion ,
.BR blast ,
and
.BR burst .
An unrecognised value selects a style at random.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
nihilurk
nihilurk bae
nihilurk -s 1234 -ns
nihilurk -content | grep -i dragon
NIHILURK_SPAWN="basilisk" nihilurk
.EE
.SH SPAWN API
The game exposes its content lookup and spawn helpers from the
.B models
crate. They are intended for tests, tools, and engine code rather than normal
play.
.TP
.BI "spawn_named(world, name, pos)"
Returns an entity for the exact, case-sensitive content name, or
.BR None .
It can spawn monsters, items, traps, and the Element of Yoord. A named spawn
uses the catalog row as written: it does not roll loot enchantments, wand
charges, or ammunition bundles.
.TP
.B content_names()
Returns every name accepted by
.BR spawn_named ();
the result is grouped by category and is the source of the
.B \-content
listing.
.TP
.BI "spawn_requested(world, near, occupied)"
Reads
.B NIHILURK_SPAWN
and places recognised names around
.IR near .
It is the convenience wrapper used during floor generation.
.TP
.BI "spawn_list(world, list, near, occupied)"
The testable form of
.BR spawn_requested .
It reads a comma-separated string directly and returns the number of names
recognised.
.PP
For random floor loot, use
.BR roll_item ();
it selects a category and row using the floor's depth and random source, then
applies that row's normal loot generation.
.PP
The Rust API is documented in
.IR docs/reference/spawn-api.md .
.SH GAME CONTROLS
.SS MOVEMENT
Move with the arrow keys, the vi keys, or the number pad. The vi and
number-pad directions are:
.PP
.EX
        y/7    k/8    u/9
        h/4           l/6
        b/1    j/2    n/3
.EE
.PP
Walk into a monster to attack it. Other useful movement commands are:
.TP
.B Shift + direction
Run in that direction until something requires attention. As nihil, with a
creature two to six tiles off that way and clear floor between, charge it
instead: one turn to close the gap and strike, and every blow that lands on
you is 25% harder until the creatures have moved. Refused with a foe already
beside you. A trap on the landing tile goes off; one jumped over does not.
.TP
.B o
Auto-explore the current floor.
.TP
.B A
Toggle picking up useful items during auto-explore.
.TP
.B O
Choose a destination and travel to it.
.TP
.B ;
Look at a tile: read what is there without acting on it.
.TP
.B Tab
Auto-fight the weakest visible foe, finishing any beside you first, or throw a boomerang or moon blade in hand at it.
.TP
.B f
Fire a matching arrow or quarrel with the launcher in hand, or throw a boomerang or moon blade in hand.
.TP
.B v
Make a reach attack with a suitable weapon, or throw a boomerang or moon blade in hand.
.SS ITEMS AND MENUS
.TP
.B i
Open the pack.
.TP
.BR a ", " t ", " d
Use, throw, or drop an item.
.TP
.B e
Equip wearable or wieldable equipment.
.TP
.BR q ", " r ", " z
Quaff a potion, read a scroll, or zap a wand.
.TP
.BR w ", " W ", " P
Wield a weapon, wear armour, or put on a ring.
.TP
.B Z
Open the spells menu, then pick a slot by its row letter. The
.B "Alt-Q, Alt-W, Alt-E, and Alt-R"
keys cast the four spell slots directly.
.SS STAIRS AND EXIT
.TP
.BR "> , ."
Go down, or walk to the down stairs.
.TP
.BR "< , ,"
Go up, or walk to the up stairs.
.TP
.B x
Close the current menu or targeting cursor without spending a turn.
.TP
.B Esc
Back out of a menu. It does not quit.
.TP
.B Q
Quit after confirmation.
.TP
.B X
Close the current interface; with nothing open, quit after confirmation.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Quit immediately. No other Ctrl key does anything.
.PP
The Alt keys need a terminal that sends Alt as a modifier. On macOS, turn on
"Use Option as Meta key" in Terminal, or set "Left Option key" to "Esc+" in
iTerm2. The
.B Z
menu works without it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR name .sav
The single save file for a run named
.IR name ,
in the directory nihilurk was started from. A won run keeps the same file as
clear data.
